  "text": "This is Pancho, he's a terrier at the Austin Animal Shelter and he's been waiting 449 days. He doesn't have a photo, nobody ever took one. He's one of 510 dogs who have an intake record here and nothing that says they left. Every one of these is real pulled from the city's open data an hour ago. The brighter the frame, the longer they've been waiting. And each one reads its own record out loud. Nothing in there is invented. Anyone who works in rescue will tell you about black dog syndrome. The idea that a dark coat keeps a dog in the kennel. So I checked it, 99,916 shelter records going back to 2013 and it isn't true. Dogs with black coats leave in 9 days. That's faster than white, faster than brown. The coats that do wait are blue and brindle and fawn, which are the coats pit bulls come in. Split them by breed and the whole thing falls out. A bully-type dog waits 28 days, every other dog waits 8. It was never the coat, it's the breed written on the kennel card. Austin's own adoption site won't tell you any of this. It'll show you a dog, but never how long he's been sitting there. This puts the two halves back together and every dog here links straight to the page where you could actually take them home. 216 of the dogs waiting tonight are pit bulls. Pancho isn't. He's a small white terrier. And he's been there 449 days.",
  "duration": 105.4
 }
